  "verbalization": "Cefadroxil, Cefditoren, and Cefepime, all cephalosporin antibiotics, share a common structural similarity and are linked through a shared lowest common ancestor in the Cephalosporin category. This suggests that these drugs may act through a common mechanism, possibly involving a shared target or pathway. The evidence points to the binding of these antibiotics to Slc22a5, a gene involved in the transport of ions and molecules across cell membranes. The recurring pattern of similarity between Cefadroxil and other cephalosporins, along with their binding to Slc22a5, implies that the interaction between these drugs and the Slc22a5 protein may be mediated by a common structural feature or functional mechanism. The integration of these paths suggests that the interaction between Cefadroxil and Slc22a5 is likely to be influenced by the structural similarities among the cephalosporin antibiotics, and that the binding of these drugs to Slc22a5 may be a shared pharmacological property.",
